GUIDES = []
GUIDES.append({"slug": "winterize-outboard-cape-cod", "title": "How to winterize an outboard on Cape Cod",
  "seo_title": "How to Winterize an Outboard on Cape Cod (Step by Step)",
  "desc": "A practical outboard winterizing guide for Cape Cod boaters: fuel, flushing, gear oil, fogging, batteries and storage, plus when to call a shop.",
  "read": "7 min", "service": "winterization-shrink-wrap",
  "html": """
<p>Cape Cod winters aren't the coldest in New England, but they swing above and below freezing over and over. That freeze-and-thaw cycle is what cracks water passages and lets moisture creep into fuel and gearcases. A good layup takes a couple of hours and saves you a lot of grief in May.</p>
<p class="callout"><strong>Before you start:</strong> your engine's owner's manual is the final word. Makers differ on fogging, fuel and stabilizer for modern fuel-injected engines. If your engine is under warranty, have a dealer do it, or keep receipts for everything you use.</p>
<h2>When to do it</h2>
<p>Most Cape boats come out between late September and November. Winterize before the first hard freeze. If you're still boating in late October, at least flush and drain the engine after each trip.</p>
<h2>What you need</h2>
<ul><li>Marine fuel stabilizer, and fresh fuel to top off</li><li>Flush muffs or a flush port adapter, and a garden hose</li><li>Lower-unit gear oil of the type your manual specifies, a pump and new drain-screw gaskets</li><li>Fogging oil, if your manual calls for it</li><li>Engine oil and filter (four-strokes), and a fuel-water separator filter</li><li>A battery charger or maintainer</li><li>Rags, a drain pan and grease for the grease points</li></ul>
<h2>Step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stabilize the fuel.</strong> Add stabilizer to the tank at the dose on the bottle, then top the tank off to about 90% to reduce condensation. Run the engine long enough to pull treated fuel through the whole system. Some owners drain portable tanks instead. Follow your manual.</li>
<li><strong>Flush with fresh water.</strong> Salt left in the cooling passages corrodes all winter. Flush on muffs or through the flush port until the water runs clean and warm. Never run the engine on muffs without water flowing, and keep clear of the prop.</li>
<li><strong>Change the engine oil and filter (four-strokes).</strong> Used oil carries acids that sit on bearings all winter. Change it now rather than in spring.</li>
<li><strong>Fog the engine, if your manual says to.</strong> Carbureted and older two-stroke engines usually get fogging oil. Many modern fuel-injected engines have their own procedure, or say not to fog through the intake. Check first.</li>
<li><strong>Change the lower-unit gear oil.</strong> Drain from the bottom screw with the top vent open. Milky or creamy oil means water got in through a seal. Get it fixed before it freezes and cracks the gearcase. Refill from the bottom until oil comes out the top vent.</li>
<li><strong>Replace the fuel-water separator.</strong> Cheap insurance against ethanol-related water problems.</li>
<li><strong>Grease and protect.</strong> Grease the swivel and tilt tubes and the steering points. Pull the prop, grease the shaft splines and check for fishing line behind the prop. Spray a corrosion inhibitor on the powerhead.</li>
<li><strong>Drain it and store it down.</strong> Lower the engine to vertical so all the water drains out of the cooling passages.</li>
<li><strong>Batteries.</strong> Charge them fully, disconnect them, and either take them home or use a maintainer. A flat battery can freeze.</li>
<li><strong>Cover it.</strong> Shrink-wrap or a good cover, with ventilation, keeps snow and moisture out.</li>
</ol>
<h2>When to call a shop</h2>
<p>Call a shop if the gear oil is milky, the tell-tale was weak all season, you found fuel in the oil, or the engine is under warranty. A shop can also handle inboard and sterndrive blocks and manifolds, and the boat's fresh-water and head systems, which take more steps to protect.</p>
<p>Want it done for you, in your driveway or at the yard? <a href="~/quote.html?service=winterizing">Send a winterizing request</a> and we'll match you with a local shop.</p>
""",
  "faq": [("Do I need to fog a fuel-injected outboard?", "It depends on the maker. Many fuel-injected four-strokes have their own storage procedure, and some advise against fogging through the intake. Check your owner's manual or ask a dealer."),
          ("Should I fill or drain the fuel tank for winter?", "For built-in tanks, most guidance is to add stabilizer and fill to about 90% to limit condensation. Portable tanks are often drained or used up. Follow your engine maker's advice.")]})
GUIDES.append({"slug": "spring-commissioning-checklist", "title": "Spring commissioning checklist for Cape Cod boaters",
  "seo_title": "Spring Boat Commissioning Checklist (Cape Cod & Islands)",
  "desc": "A printable spring commissioning checklist for Cape Cod and Islands boats: hull, engine, electrical, plumbing, safety gear, paperwork and launch day.",
  "read": "6 min", "service": "spring-commissioning",
  "html": """
<p>On Cape Cod, the season starts in a rush. Yards launch boats in a set order, harbors set moorings, and Memorial Day comes fast. Work through this list, or hand it to your shop, and your first trip won't end in a tow.</p>
<h2>Paperwork and permits</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Massachusetts registration or federal documentation is current, and the decals are on</li><li>Town mooring or slip permit renewed, and any required mooring inspection done</li><li>Insurance active, with the cruising area covering where you'll go (the Islands, for example)</li><li>Towing membership renewed, if you have one</li></ul>
<h2>Hull and deck</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Shrink-wrap off and recycled where a program exists</li><li>Hull checked for cracks, blisters and impact damage from winter storage</li><li>Bottom paint touched up or redone. Most antifouling has a minimum and maximum time before launch, so check the can.</li><li>Zincs and anodes replaced if they're more than half worn</li><li>Drain plug in. Write it on your launch checklist.</li><li>Cleats, rails and canvas checked for loose fasteners</li></ul>
<h2>Engine and drive</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Water-pump impeller checked or replaced on your maker's schedule</li><li>Gear oil checked for water, and engine oil and filters done if not done in the fall</li><li>Fuel lines, primer bulb and clamps checked for cracking</li><li>Fuel-water separator filter replaced</li><li>Belts, hoses and clamps checked (inboards and sterndrives), and sterndrive bellows checked</li><li>Steering and trim worked lock to lock, and fluid levels checked</li><li>First start on muffs or in the water: strong tell-tale stream, no alarms</li></ul>
<h2>Electrical</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Batteries charged, load-tested and reinstalled with clean, tight terminals</li><li>Navigation lights, horn, bilge pumps and float switches tested</li><li>Electronics updated, and the VHF radio checked (with DSC and your MMSI entered)</li></ul>
<h2>Plumbing</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Through-hulls and seacocks opened and closed, and hose clamps checked (double-clamp below the waterline)</li><li>Fresh-water system flushed of antifreeze</li><li>Head and holding tank checked</li></ul>
<h2>Safety gear</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Wearable life jackets for everyone, in sizes that fit, plus a throwable on boats 16 feet and longer</li><li>Visual distress signals in date, plus a sound-producing device</li><li>Fire extinguisher, if your boat requires one, checked and in date</li><li>Anchor, chain and rode suited to the depths where you'll anchor</li><li>First-aid kit, a flashlight and a paper chart of your area</li></ul>
<h2>Launch day</h2>
<ul class="checks"><li>Check the drain plug again</li><li>Run the blower before starting gas inboards</li><li>Watch the bilge for the first hour in the water</li><li>Short shakedown run before the first long trip</li></ul>
<p>Requirements change, so check the current U.S. Coast Guard equipment rules for your boat's size and type.</p>
<p>Rather have a pro do it? <a href="~/quote.html?service=commissioning">Request spring commissioning</a>. In Cape harbors, earlier is better.</p>
""",
  "faq": [("When should I book commissioning on Cape Cod?", "In February or March if you want to launch in May. Calendars fill up before Memorial Day."),
          ("How often should I replace the water-pump impeller?", "Follow your engine maker's schedule. Many suggest every few seasons or a set number of hours. Running in sandy, shallow water wears them out sooner.")]})
GUIDES.append({"slug": "bottom-paint-cape-cod-waters", "title": "Choosing bottom paint for Cape Cod and Islands waters",
  "seo_title": "Choosing Bottom Paint for Cape Cod Waters: Ablative vs Hard",
  "desc": "How to pick antifouling bottom paint on Cape Cod, the Vineyard and Nantucket: ablative or hard, copper or copper-free, aluminum hulls and local growing conditions.",
  "read": "6 min", "service": "bottom-paint",
  "html": """
<p>There's no single best bottom paint. The right one depends on how warm and shallow your harbor is, how long the boat stays in the water, how often you run it, and what the hull and drive are made of.</p>
<h2>1. How you use the boat</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Trailered and hauled after every trip:</strong> you may not need antifouling at all. Rinse and dry the boat after each trip.</li><li><strong>On a mooring or slip all season, used often:</strong> <em>ablative</em> (self-polishing) paints wear slowly as the boat moves through the water, exposing fresh biocide. It's a popular choice on the Cape.</li><li><strong>Sits for long stretches, or gets scrubbed or raced:</strong> <em>hard</em> (modified epoxy) paints leave a tougher, scrubbable surface, but they build up layers over the years.</li></ul>
<h2>2. Your water</h2>
<p>Warm, shallow, protected bays such as Waquoit, Popponesset, Pleasant Bay, Lewis Bay and the salt ponds often grow slime, weed and barnacles faster in July and August than deeper, cooler, current-swept water in Vineyard Sound, Buzzards Bay or off the Canal. Boats on warm-water moorings usually need stronger protection, or a mid-season haul and scrub.</p>
<h2>3. Hull and drive material</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Fiberglass:</strong> most paints work. New or blistered hulls benefit from an epoxy <em>barrier coat</em> first.</li><li><strong>Aluminum hulls, outdrives and outboard lower units:</strong> <strong>don't</strong> use cuprous-oxide (copper) paint directly on aluminum. It can cause galvanic corrosion. Use a paint labeled for aluminum, usually copper-free.</li><li><strong>Wood:</strong> ask a yard with wooden-boat experience. Some traditional boats use different systems.</li></ul>
<h2>4. Compatibility</h2>
<p>Different paint types don't always stick to each other. Putting ablative over hard paint, or a new chemistry over old layers, may need a sealer, a primer or stripping. Know what's on your bottom now. Your yard's records or an old can are the easiest way to find out.</p>
<h2>5. The environment</h2>
<p>Antifouling works by being toxic to growth, so it matters how it's applied and removed. Professional yards contain sanding dust and wash water. Copper-free and lower-copper options keep improving. Ask your yard about them, especially if you're near shellfish beds or a sensitive pond.</p>
<h2>Quick answers</h2>
<ul><li>Most Cape mooring boats: an ablative paint suited to the region, applied over the right primer.</li><li>Aluminum anything: an aluminum-safe paint, copper-free.</li><li>Racing or frequently scrubbed: a hard paint, burnished.</li></ul>
<p>Want a yard to recommend and apply it? <a href="~/quote.html?service=bottom_paint">Send a bottom-paint request</a> with your boat, hull material and harbor.</p>
""",
  "faq": [("How often does bottom paint need redoing on Cape Cod?", "Many mooring boats are recoated every season or two, depending on the paint and how fast the boat grows. Your yard can tell you from the condition at haul-out."),
          ("Can I launch right after painting?", "Every paint has a minimum dry time, and many have a maximum time before launch. Follow the can and your yard's schedule.")]})
GUIDES.append({"slug": "outboard-wont-start", "title": "Outboard won't start? Check these first",
  "seo_title": "Outboard Won't Start? 12 Checks Before You Call a Mechanic",
  "desc": "Twelve quick, safe checks when your outboard won't start or dies: kill switch, fuel, primer bulb, vent, battery, neutral and more, plus when to call a mechanic.",
  "read": "5 min", "service": "engine-repair",
  "html": """
<p>Many no-starts are something simple. Work through these in order. They're quick, and most need no tools. If you're on the water and drifting toward danger, anchor first. If anyone is at risk, call the Coast Guard on VHF 16.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Kill-switch lanyard.</strong> Is the clip on the switch? It's the most common no-start there is.</li>
<li><strong>In neutral.</strong> Most engines won't crank in gear. Wiggle the shifter in neutral.</li>
<li><strong>Tank vent open.</strong> A closed vent on a portable tank starves the engine after a few minutes of running.</li>
<li><strong>Fuel in the tank, and the right tank selected.</strong> Fuel gauges lie. Look or dip the tank.</li>
<li><strong>Primer bulb.</strong> Squeeze until it's firm. If it never firms up, the fitting or bulb may be leaking or the line may be kinked.</li>
<li><strong>Fuel line connections.</strong> Check that quick-connects click home at both ends.</li>
<li><strong>Battery switch and battery.</strong> Is the switch on, and are the terminals tight and clean? Slow cranking usually means a weak battery or a bad connection.</li>
<li><strong>Fuses and breakers.</strong> A blown fuse can kill the starter or ignition.</li>
<li><strong>Choke or prime</strong> (older engines). Follow the cold-start procedure for your engine.</li>
<li><strong>Flooded?</strong> If you smell fuel, wait a few minutes and try again with the throttle slightly open, if your manual allows that.</li>
<li><strong>Water in the fuel.</strong> Look at the fuel-water separator bowl. Water or debris there points to fuel trouble.</li>
<li><strong>Alarms.</strong> Note any beeps or warning lights. They're clues for the mechanic.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Stop and call a mechanic if…</h2>
<ul><li>You hear grinding, knocking or a clunk</li><li>There's no tell-tale water stream after starting (shut it down)</li><li>The overheat alarm sounds</li><li>Fuel is leaking anywhere</li><li>It starts and then dies again and again</li></ul>
<p>Write down what happened, the engine make and model, and the hours, and take a short video if you can. Then <a href="~/quote.html?service=engine_outboard">send an engine repair request</a>. Mobile shops can often check it at your slip or mooring.</p>
""",
  "faq": [("Why does my outboard start and then die?", "Common causes are a closed tank vent, a fuel-line air leak, a clogged filter, water in the fuel or an idle problem. If the simple checks don't fix it, have a mechanic check the fuel system."),
          ("Is it safe to run an outboard on muffs?", "Yes, with full water flow before you start. Keep clear of the prop, and don't rev the engine. Shut it down at once if the tell-tale stream stops.")]})

